module product.fabric.supply

// A SupplierOffer is a supplier's statement of capacity it is willing to sell. Owned
// capacity and rented capacity are ONE type: what differs is the evidence behind
// the statement, not the kind of thing being stated.

// Observed and quoted supply differ in their evidence, not in their type, and
// minting two SupplierOffer types would fork the selection fold that must compare
// them. ObservedSupply is a measurement - this host has three free slots right
// now, and here is the receipt. QuotedSupply is a standing willingness to sell
// - this supplier will provide up to N of this shape at this rate until this
// time, and here is the quote. Bottomless cloud supply is a QuotedSupply with
// a large quantity bound, never an unbounded one: an offer without a bound
// cannot be conserved against, and infinite supply is a claim no supplier
// actually makes. The fields the decision consumes - quantity bound,
// readiness, validity, normalized quote - stay SURFACED on the offer rather
// than hidden inside the evidence; the evidence proves those values, it does
// not replace them.
// An owned offer carries an EXPLICIT ZERO quote and creates a zero-valued
// money reservation, rather than carrying no quote at all. Absence must never
// be interpreted as free. The predecessor model made cost_quote optional and
// every owned host used the absent arm, so a consumer reading it could not
// distinguish costs nothing from nobody priced this - and the safe reading of
// the second is not zero. Requiring the amount means a supplier whose price is
// genuinely unknown has to say so in a way the selection fold can refuse,
// instead of being silently treated as the cheapest offer available.
// A quote is std.measure's MoneyRate at the period the supplier bills in,
// never a second money-per-period record. MoneyRate already carries the amount
// and the currency and already makes the period a type argument, so a rate at
// one period is not assignable where another is expected -- which is exactly
// the property a price needs, and the reason an earlier version of this file
// minting its own carrier was a fork rather than a specialization. That
// version's stated defence was that folding the unit into the measure would
// make two rates addable; MoneyRate does not fold it into the measure, so the
// defence described a carrier that does not exist.
//
// The runtime coproduct is here for a reason the type parameter cannot serve:
// selection ranks offers billed on different bases against each other, so they
// must inhabit one roster, and SupplierOffer<P, PerHour> and SupplierOffer<P, PerSecond> would
// be different types. Discriminating at the value level keeps the roster
// homogeneous while keeping the periods unaddable, since reaching either
// amount requires naming which period it is.
//
// Normalizing a supplier's published terms into one of these is the supplier
// binding's work, and so are billing quantum, rounding, minimum charge, fixed
// and setup charges, caps, and when billing starts and stops. The core
// compares normalized quotes; it does not model any supplier's tariff.

type OfferQuote =
  | QuotedPerSecond(MoneyPerSecond)
  | QuotedPerMinute(MoneyPerMinute)
  | QuotedPerHour(MoneyPerHour)
  | QuotedFlatPerGrant(MoneyOnce)

fn offer_quote_amount(q: OfferQuote) -> std.measure.MoneyAmountMicro {
  match q {
    QuotedPerSecond(r) => r.amount
    QuotedPerMinute(r) => r.amount
    QuotedPerHour(r) => r.amount
    QuotedFlatPerGrant(r) => r.amount
  }
}

fn offer_quote_currency(q: OfferQuote) -> std.currency.CurrencyCode {
  match q {
    QuotedPerSecond(r) => r.currency
    QuotedPerMinute(r) => r.currency
    QuotedPerHour(r) => r.currency
    QuotedFlatPerGrant(r) => r.currency
  }
}

type OfferEvidence
  = ObservedSupply { observed_at: std.types.Timestamp, receipt: product.fabric.demand.ObservationReceiptRef }
  | QuotedSupply {
      quoted_at: std.types.Timestamp,
      valid_until: std.types.Timestamp,
      receipt: product.fabric.demand.ObservationReceiptRef,
    }

type SupplierOffer<P> {
  id: FabricIdentity<P, OfferKey>
  executor: P
  shape: Shape
  capabilities: CapabilityManifestRef
  trust_domain: TrustDomainRef
  isolation: IsolationProfile
  quantity_bound: Int
  ready_at: std.types.Timestamp
  quote: OfferQuote
  billing_quantum: Second
  evidence: OfferEvidence
}

// A SupplierOffer carries no remaining_capacity field. Remaining capacity is DERIVED
// as the allocatable bound minus the sum of active grant claims against that
// offer, and deriving it is precisely what makes atomic grant issuance
// conserve supply. A stored remainder is a second representation of the grant
// set: it can disagree with the grants that actually exist, and the
// disagreement is invisible until two grants have already been issued against
// capacity that existed only in a stale field.
// Selection is a named policy with one implementation. The interface is named so
// a second policy is a second instantiation rather than an edit to ExecutionGrant or to
// the clearing fold; if adding one later requires touching a core carrier, the
// axis was never real.

// The policy is an axis with exactly one instantiation today: cheapest
// fungible with delay valuation. The ORDER of its filters is load-bearing and
// is not a performance detail. Fungibility is decided FIRST - which offers can
// satisfy this work's shape, capabilities and trust domain, with price not
// consulted - so that price can never rescue an offer that cannot run the
// work. Affordability is decided SECOND: within the demand's reservation price
// and maximum buy order, with budget remaining. Cheapest is decided LAST, over
// the survivors, comparing provider charge plus valued availability delay,
// with a stable deterministic tie-break. This is a posted-offer market and not
// an auction: suppliers publish asks, each demand carries a buyer reservation
// price, and the fabric takes the cheapest admissible ask. No clearing-price
// machinery exists because none is needed to make that decision.
// Rented supply being selected only after owned supply is exhausted is a
// CONSEQUENCE of cheapest-admissible-ask, never a fallback arm, and the
// distinction decides whether cost can run away. A fallback is a failure arm
// that widens when the preferred path is unavailable, and it hides the deficit
// that triggered it. Here nothing widens: owned capacity at an explicit zero
// quote is simply the cheapest admissible ask while any remains, and when none
// remains the same fold evaluates the same predicate over the remaining asks.
// If no ask is admissible - budget exhausted, every ask above the reservation
// price or the maximum buy order, no offer fungible - the outcome is a typed
// refusal or a demand that stays pending. It is never a degraded execution
// path, and there is no arm to reach for one.

type SupplySelectionPolicy =
  | CheapestFungibleWithDelayValuation

// FUNGIBILITY IS THE FABRIC'S QUESTION, NOT A CONSUMER'S.
//
// This fold arrived here from gunbc.fabric_witness_run, where it had been
// written as authorize_floor_run with a FloorRunRefusal coproduct. Nothing in
// it was ever about the floor: comparing a Work's requirements against an
// SupplierOffer's capabilities is provider-neutral, and work.dag's own header already
// calls fungibility "selection's first and load-bearing step". A consumer that
// owns this fold is a consumer that will answer it differently from the next
// consumer, which is the fork the fabric exists to prevent.
//
// FUNGIBILITY AND AFFORDABILITY ARE TWO FACTS WITH TWO HOMES, NOT ONE VERDICT.
// A demand that cannot afford an offer and a demand an offer cannot serve are
// different facts with different remedies -- raise the buy order, or find
// another supplier. Per DESIGN section 3, fungibility is a SUPPLIER
// CAPABILITY fact (does this executor satisfy this work's shape, trust domain
// and capabilities -- true regardless of who is asking or what they will pay)
// and affordability is BUYER POLICY (can this demand's ceiling, in this
// currency, clear this supplier's asking price -- true regardless of whether
// the executor can even run the work). An earlier revision fused both into
// one OfferEligibility coproduct decided by one offer_eligibility_for fold
// taking a budget ceiling as an argument, so "this offer cannot run your job"
// and "this offer is currently too expensive" were the same verdict wearing
// different constructors. Two coproducts and two folds below, each named for
// the question it answers and neither importing the other's inputs:
// offer_fungibility_for takes no money at all, and offer_affordability_for
// takes no shape, trust domain or capabilities.

type OfferFungibility
  = Fungible
  | TrustDomainMismatch { required: TrustDomainRef, offered: TrustDomainRef }
  | CapabilitiesNotOffered { required: CapabilityManifestRef, offered: CapabilityManifestRef }
  | IsolationNotProvided { missing: List<IsolationGuarantee> }
  | ShapeAxesNotCovered { unmet: List<UnmetShapeAxis> }

// ShapeNotCovered IS DELETED RATHER THAN KEPT BESIDE ITS REPLACEMENT.
//
// It carried exactly one axis -- required and offered thread counts -- which ShapeAxesNotCovered
// now carries as ThreadsNotCovered alongside the axes it could not express. Nothing constructs it
// any more, so keeping it would leave a variant every consumer must match and no producer can
// ever emit: a permanently-unreachable arm that reads as coverage. DESIGN section 4b(4) is
// explicit that a climb deletes the redundant lower-rung machinery it obsoletes rather than
// accumulating it beside the replacement.

// GRANT DURATION -- the fact a RATE quote needs before its per-unit price can
// be compared to a per-grant ceiling AT ALL. "I cannot compare a $/hr rate to
// a total ceiling" is true, but it is a UNITS problem -- ignorance about the
// comparison -- not a fact about the offer, and rendering that ignorance as a
// verdict on the supplier is the state-space conflation DESIGN section 5
// names (an offer that is capable, in-currency and cheap must not be
// unconditionally ineligible because the fold never received what it needed
// to price it). The repair supplies the missing duration; it does not refuse
// the supplier.
//
// EXACT and ESTIMATED are not decoration on top of that repair -- they are
// the other half of it. Multiplying a rate by an estimated duration produces
// a PROJECTED total whose error is inherited from the estimate, so
// "affordable under a projected total" is a different fact from "affordable
// under a known one", and collapsing the two into a bare Second would let an
// estimate's uncertainty vanish the moment it is multiplied. A caller that
// only knows an estimate cannot construct ExactGrantDuration and claim
// otherwise -- the two arms are the wall, not a comment beside a single field.
type GrantDuration
  = ExactGrantDuration { seconds: std.measure.Second }
  | EstimatedGrantDuration { seconds: std.measure.Second }

fn grant_duration_seconds(d: GrantDuration) -> std.measure.Second {
  match d {
    ExactGrantDuration { seconds: s } => s
    EstimatedGrantDuration { seconds: s } => s
  }
}

type DemandOfferAffordability
  = Affordable
  | AffordableUnderEstimate { duration_seconds: std.measure.Second, projected_total: std.measure.MoneyAmountMicro }
  | QuoteExceedsMaximumBuyOrder { quoted: std.measure.MoneyAmountMicro, maximum: std.measure.MoneyAmountMicro }
  | QuoteCurrencyMismatch { quoted: std.currency.CurrencyCode, budgeted: std.currency.CurrencyCode }

fn offer_covers_shape(offered: Shape, needed: Shape) -> Bool {
  (unmet_shape_axes(offered: offered, needed: needed) |> count) == 0
}

// THREADS WERE THE ONLY AXIS COMPARED, SO FUNGIBILITY RETURNED WRONG ANSWERS.
//
// An 8 GiB x64 workload was declared fungible with a 6 GiB arm offer whenever thread counts and the
// textual capability ref matched. The broker would route work to a machine that cannot run it, and
// nothing downstream refuses because fungibility already said yes.
//
// EACH UNMET AXIS IS NAMED, FOR THE REASON THE ISOLATION MATCH GIVES: "does not fit" is
// unactionable, while "needs 8589934592 bytes, offered 6442450944" tells a broker which supplier
// class to look for. An axis the WORK does not state is skipped and is not reported as checked --
// unstated is not satisfied-by-everything, and rendering it as covered would turn absence of a
// requirement into a positive clearance.
//
// ARCHITECTURE IS EQUALITY, NOT ORDER. There is no sense in which x86_64 "covers" aarch64: a
// binary built for one does not run on the other, so the comparison is a match and a mismatch is
// total. Threads and memory are genuine at-least comparisons.
type UnmetShapeAxis
  = ThreadsNotCovered { required: HardwareThreadCount, offered: HardwareThreadCount }
  | MemoryNotCovered { required: ByteSize, offered: ByteSize }
  | MemoryNotOffered { required: ByteSize }
  | ArchitectureMismatch { required: Architecture, offered: Architecture }
  | ArchitectureNotOffered { required: Architecture }

fn unmet_shape_axis_wire(axis: UnmetShapeAxis) -> String {
  match axis {
    ThreadsNotCovered { required: r, offered: o } =>
      join(["threads: needs ", to_string(hardware_thread_count_value(t: r)),
            ", offered ", to_string(hardware_thread_count_value(t: o))], "")
    MemoryNotCovered { required: r, offered: o } =>
      join(["memory: needs ", to_string(byte_size_count(b: r)),
            " bytes, offered ", to_string(byte_size_count(b: o))], "")
    MemoryNotOffered { required: r } =>
      join(["memory: needs ", to_string(byte_size_count(b: r)),
            " bytes, offer states none"], "")
    ArchitectureMismatch { required: r, offered: o } =>
      join(["architecture: needs ", architecture_diagnostic_name(arch: r) as String,
            ", offered ", architecture_diagnostic_name(arch: o) as String], "")
    ArchitectureNotOffered { required: r } =>
      join(["architecture: needs ", architecture_diagnostic_name(arch: r) as String,
            ", offer states none"], "")
  }
}

fn unmet_thread_axis(offered: Shape, needed: Shape) -> List<UnmetShapeAxis> {
  if hardware_thread_count_value(t: offered.hard.threads)
    >= hardware_thread_count_value(t: needed.hard.threads) {
    []
  } else {
    [ThreadsNotCovered { required: needed.hard.threads, offered: offered.hard.threads }]
  }
}

fn unmet_memory_axis(offered: Shape, needed: Shape) -> List<UnmetShapeAxis> {
  match needed.envelope.memory {
    Absent => []
    Present { value: want } =>
      match offered.envelope.memory {
        Absent => [MemoryNotOffered { required: want.min_bytes }]
        Present { value: have } =>
          if byte_size_count(b: have.min_bytes) >= byte_size_count(b: want.min_bytes) {
            []
          } else {
            [MemoryNotCovered { required: want.min_bytes, offered: have.min_bytes }]
          }
      }
  }
}

fn unmet_architecture_axis(offered: Shape, needed: Shape) -> List<UnmetShapeAxis> {
  match needed.envelope.cpu {
    Absent => []
    Present { value: want_cpu } =>
      match want_cpu.architecture {
        Absent => []
        Present { value: want } =>
          match offered.envelope.cpu {
            Absent => [ArchitectureNotOffered { required: want }]
            Present { value: have_cpu } =>
              match have_cpu.architecture {
                Absent => [ArchitectureNotOffered { required: want }]
                Present { value: have } =>
                  if have == want { [] } else {
                    [ArchitectureMismatch { required: want, offered: have }]
                  }
              }
          }
      }
  }
}

fn unmet_shape_axes(offered: Shape, needed: Shape) -> List<UnmetShapeAxis> {
  concat(
    unmet_thread_axis(offered: offered, needed: needed),
    concat(
      unmet_memory_axis(offered: offered, needed: needed),
      unmet_architecture_axis(offered: offered, needed: needed),
    ),
  )
}

// THIS FOLD SCREENS FUNGIBILITY. IT DOES NOT SELECT, AND IT TAKES NO MONEY.
//
// It answers only: can this executor's shape, trust domain and capabilities
// satisfy what this work requires. Nothing here chooses among several
// fungible offers, and choosing -- like pricing -- is where economics live,
// never here.

fn offer_fungibility_for<P>(requirements: ExecutionRequirements, offer: SupplierOffer<P>) -> OfferFungibility {
  let missing_isolation = unsatisfied_isolation_guarantees(
    required: requirements.isolation,
    provided: offer.isolation,
  )
  let unmet_axes = unmet_shape_axes(offered: offer.shape, needed: requirements.shape)
  if requirements.trust_domain != offer.trust_domain {
    TrustDomainMismatch { required: requirements.trust_domain, offered: offer.trust_domain }
  } else if requirements.capabilities != offer.capabilities {
    CapabilitiesNotOffered { required: requirements.capabilities, offered: offer.capabilities }
  } else if (missing_isolation |> count) > 0 {
    IsolationNotProvided { missing: missing_isolation }
  } else if (unmet_axes |> count) > 0 {
    ShapeAxesNotCovered { unmet: unmet_axes }
  } else {
    Fungible
  }
}

fn seconds_per_hour() -> std.nat.Nat {
  std.measure.minutes_per_hour() * std.measure.seconds_per_minute()
}

// A BILLING QUANTUM: the smallest interval a supplier ever charges for, and
// suppliers round UP a partial quantum rather than pro-rating it. It is a
// PER-SUPPLIER FACT and is carried on the offer, because it is not a property
// the fabric may choose. Hetzner bills a one-hour minimum rounded up, so one
// second of use costs an hour; Ubicloud bills by the minute; owned capacity
// bills nothing at all and is therefore CONTINUOUS.
//
// A quantum of ZERO means continuous, and that is not a degenerate case to be
// tolerated -- it is the modeled advantage of owned compute. A 90-second job on
// an hourly supplier is billed 3600 seconds and wastes 3510 of them; the same
// job on continuous capacity is billed 90. For short CI work that waste can
// exceed the entire difference in headline rate, so a fold that compared rates
// while assuming one quantum for everybody would rank suppliers by the wrong
// number and never say why.
//
// An earlier revision hard-coded a SYNTHETIC one-hour quantum here for every
// offer, and cited gunbc-private's richer per-supplier tariff by name as the
// thing it was standing in for. That stand-in silently overcharged every
// sub-hourly supplier by up to a full hour per grant: normalizing a per-minute
// vendor into the per-hour arm made a three-minute job project a whole hour, a
// twentyfold overstatement that would have kept the selection fold from ever
// choosing that supplier. The quantum is now the supplier's own.
fn billed_unit_count(seconds: Nat, quantum_seconds: Nat) -> Nat {
  if quantum_seconds == 0 {
    seconds
  } else {
    (seconds + (quantum_seconds - 1)) / quantum_seconds
  }
}

// THE PROJECTED PER-GRANT TOTAL a quote would bill over one grant of this
// duration. QuotedFlatPerGrant already IS a per-grant total, so duration does
// not multiply it -- doing so would double-count a grant the quote already
// prices whole. QuotedPerSecond and QuotedPerHour are RATES, so the total is
// the rate times the duration expressed in that rate's own unit.
//
// QuotedPerSecond rounds UP via measure_scale_fraction_ceil, the SAME
// ceiling authority std.measure already uses for scale conversion. QuotedPerHour
// rounds up to whole BILLED HOURS first (billed_unit_count) and only then
// multiplies by the hourly rate -- a coarser rounding than a fractional-hour
// ceiling, because that is how the supplier actually bills. Rounding down
// anywhere here would understate the bill and could admit an offer whose real
// cost exceeds the ceiling, which is the absorbing-fallback failure (DESIGN
// section 5) one level down: a refusal arm that quietly narrows instead of
// refusing.
fn billed_seconds(seconds: Nat, quantum_seconds: Nat) -> Nat {
  if quantum_seconds == 0 {
    seconds
  } else {
    billed_unit_count(seconds: seconds, quantum_seconds: quantum_seconds) * quantum_seconds
  }
}

// The quantum arrives as a Second rather than a bare Nat for the same reason
// GrantDuration does, two declarations up: this file already models every other
// duration it carries as Second, so a *_seconds: Nat field here was the one place
// the magnitude's name lived in the field name instead of in its type. Both are
// unwrapped to counts on the next two lines because what follows is integer
// ceiling arithmetic over unit counts, and a count scaled by measure_scale_fraction_ceil
// is a dimensionless multiplier, not a duration.
fn offer_quoted_total_for_grant(
  quote: OfferQuote,
  duration: GrantDuration,
  quantum: Second,
) -> MoneyAmountMicro {
  let seconds = second_count(s: grant_duration_seconds(d: duration))
  let billed = billed_seconds(seconds: seconds, quantum_seconds: second_count(s: quantum))
  match quote {
    QuotedFlatPerGrant(r) => r.amount
    QuotedPerSecond(r) => measure_scale_fraction_ceil(m: r.amount, num: billed, den: 1)
    QuotedPerMinute(r) =>
      measure_scale_fraction_ceil(m: r.amount, num: billed, den: seconds_per_minute())
    QuotedPerHour(r) =>
      measure_scale_fraction_ceil(m: r.amount, num: billed, den: seconds_per_hour())
  }
}

// THIS FOLD SCREENS AFFORDABILITY. IT DOES NOT SELECT, AND IT TAKES NO SHAPE,
// TRUST DOMAIN OR CAPABILITIES.
//
// It answers: can this demand pay this offer's asking price, in this
// currency, for one grant of the given duration. Nothing here chooses among
// several affordable offers, and choosing is where the economics actually
// live.
//
// WHAT AN OFFER CARRIES, AND WHAT IT DOES NOT. The quote is a supplier-side
// ASKING PRICE for one grant. It is NOT an opportunity cost: what an hour could
// otherwise have earned depends on which OTHER demands could use it, so it is a
// property of the assignment evaluation and of the decision receipt, never a
// field on the supply row. Two earlier revisions of this note got this wrong in
// opposite directions -- first calling opportunity cost a demand-side question,
// then carrying it on the offer as a second cost -- and both are recorded here
// rather than reworded, because the sentence was cited downstream each time.
//
// Nor is marginal cash cost zero for an owned host: power and cooling are
// incurred by the act of running, so they are a real dispatch input. Only the
// HISTORICAL PURCHASE is sunk, and sunk cost is excluded from dispatch while
// being retained for accounting.
//
// WHAT SELECTION WOULD NEED, none of which exists here: the offer's availability
// interval, the SUPPLIER'S OWN tariff (offer_quoted_total_for_grant's
// billed_unit_count is a synthetic one-hour quantum for the affordability
// screen, not a per-supplier tariff row), the paid-through commitment state
// that makes an already-bought hour's marginal cash zero until it expires,
// transition and start costs, and the alternative uses that give an
// opportunity cost its value -- including the arm where there is NO feasible
// alternative use, so an hour about to expire idle is not priced as though a
// customer had been displaced, and the arm where the alternative is simply
// UNREAD, so unknown does not silently become zero.
//
// Until those land, this fold is honest as a screen and would be a lie as a
// selector.

// The budget ceiling arrives as SCALARS rather than as demand.BuyTerms, because
// supply must not import demand -- a backward edge this module already carries
// once for ObservationReceiptRef and must not deepen. The demand side supplies
// them at the call site, which is also where they are known. The duration is a
// third scalar for the same reason: it is a fact about the GRANT being priced,
// known to whoever is asking, never stored on the offer.

fn offer_affordability_for<P>(
  offer: SupplierOffer<P>,
  maximum_buy_order: MoneyAmountMicro,
  budget_currency: CurrencyCode,
  duration: GrantDuration,
) -> DemandOfferAffordability {
  if offer_quote_currency(q: offer.quote) != budget_currency {
    QuoteCurrencyMismatch { quoted: offer_quote_currency(q: offer.quote), budgeted: budget_currency }
  } else {
    let projected = offer_quoted_total_for_grant(quote: offer.quote, duration: duration, quantum: offer.billing_quantum)
    if money_amount_micro_count(m: projected) > money_amount_micro_count(m: maximum_buy_order) {
      QuoteExceedsMaximumBuyOrder { quoted: projected, maximum: maximum_buy_order }
    } else {
      match duration {
        ExactGrantDuration { seconds: _ } => Affordable
        EstimatedGrantDuration { seconds: s } => match offer.quote {
          QuotedFlatPerGrant(_) => Affordable
          QuotedPerSecond(_) =>
            AffordableUnderEstimate { duration_seconds: s, projected_total: projected }
          QuotedPerMinute(_) =>
            AffordableUnderEstimate { duration_seconds: s, projected_total: projected }
          QuotedPerHour(_) =>
            AffordableUnderEstimate { duration_seconds: s, projected_total: projected }
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
